In article <GRABINER.93Feb21120256@boucher.harvard.edu> grabiner@math.harvard.edu (David Grabiner) writes:
>In article <FLEE.93Feb17024056@dictionopolis.cs.psu.edu>, Felix Lee writes:
>> Hmm.  How about: autopickup should never pick things up if you're
>> overburdened?
>In particular, if there are 100 rocks on the floor, you might want to
>pick up ten, or as many as you can carry without being encumbered.
>There is currently no way to do this without picking up the whole pile
>and then dropping some back.

After some experimenting in wizard mode, I have found this to be incorrect.
If there is more than one type of item in the square, just hit comma and
then A, and use the # modifier to pick up a particular number of items.

If there is only one group of objects in the square--100 rocks, for example--
then just type "10,".  I wasn't sure that the comma command would deal
gracefully with a number argument, but it does.  My compliments to the Dev
Team.

